Namely, (1) the
supreme and good God; (2) His Christ; (3) the space in which He
dwells; (4) the matter of His creation; (5) the Demiurge
(or Marcion’s “Creator”); (6) his promised Christ;
(7) the space which contains him; (8) this world, his creation;
(9) evil, inherent in it.

Nazaræos;
or, Nazarites. [Christians were still so called by the Jews in
the Third Century. Kaye, 446.]

He hath killed,
hath power to cast into hell” (meaning, of course, the Creator);
“yea, I say unto you, fear Him.” Luke xii. 5.

Gerunt: although
vainly at present (“jam vana in Judæis”—Oehler);
Semler conjectures “gemunt, bewail.”
